# Line endings are load-bearing here, in both directions.

# cmd.exe mis-parses a batch file whose lines end in LF alone — it was one of
# the sharp edges found on real hardware, and the fix was to ship CRLF. `-text`
# rather than `eol=crlf` so git stores and delivers the exact bytes and never
# converts on anyone's checkout, whatever their core.autocrlf is set to.
*.cmd   -text

# PowerShell is less fragile about LF, but these run beside the .cmd on the
# same Windows machines and there is no reason for them to differ.
*.ps1   -text

# The other direction: bash fails on a CRLF script with errors that name the
# carriage return in ways nobody recognises ("$'\r': command not found").
*.sh    text eol=lf
